==English==

===Etymology===
From Old (and modern) French {{term|solliciter}}, from Latin {{term|sollicitare||stir, disturb, later look after}}, from {{term|sollicitus||[[agitate]]d, anxious, punctilious, literally 'thoroughly moved'}}, from {{term|sollus||whole, entire}} + past participle of {{term|ciere||to shake, excite, cite, to put in motion}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|sə-lĭs'ĭt}}
* {{IPA| /səˈlɪsɪt/}}
* {{audio|en-us-solicit.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|ɪsɪt}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To persistently [[endeavor]] to obtain an object, or [[bring about]] an event.
# To [[woo]]; to [[court]].
# To persuade or [[incite]] one to commit some act, especially illegal or sexual behavior.
# To offer to perform sexual activity, especially when for a [[payment]].
# To make a [[petition]].
# {{archaic}} To [[disturb]] or trouble; to [[harass]].

